<http://data.silknow.org/object/39cf5e18-3e3d-3f46-9384-3ef517cd2b70>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "embroidered linen, 1450-1499, Flemish"@en , "Cope hood embroidered with couched gold thread, for upper background, and long, short and basket stitching over gold threads for the foreground.\n\nChrist is shown face-on flanked by the two thieves, who are blindfold. In the background are two smiling angels in flight bearing chalices. To the lower-right of the scene are three figures on horseback, one of whom may be a priest as he wears a headdress and a Greek(?) symbol on his chest. To the lower-left stand The Virgin Mary, St. John and Mary Magdalene, with two other mourning women. At the bottom, three soldiers quarrel over Christ's clothes. The soldiers are dressed in High German costume, as are the other two horsemen. All figures are embroidered on canvas in long and short stitches and applied to the ground.\n\nThe object has been backed with a later (18th century?) silk."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/2ae881db-25cf-345f-8330-3ad272be9069> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Cope hood embroidered with couched gold thread, for upper background, and long, short and basket stitching over gold threads for the foreground.\n\nChrist is shown face-on flanked by the two thieves, who are blindfold. In the background are two smiling angels in flight bearing chalices. To the lower-right of the scene are three figures on horseback, one of whom may be a priest as he wears a headdress and a Greek(?) symbol on his chest. To the lower-left stand The Virgin Mary, St. John and Mary Magdalene, with two other mourning women. At the bottom, three soldiers quarrel over Christ's clothes. The soldiers are dressed in High German costume, as are the other two horsemen. All figures are embroidered on canvas in long and short stitches and applied to the ground.\n\nThe object has been backed with a later (18th century?) silk."@en , "embroidered linen, 1450-1499, Flemish"@en ;
